{
  "result": "The town had a clock that ran seven minutes slow. Nobody remembered when it had started losing time, and eventually nobody minded. Trains still arrived according to their own schedules, bread rose at its usual pace, and the sun ignored every clock entirely.\n\nMara, who repaired bicycles beside the square, liked the mistake. She said it gave everyone seven extra minutes to change their minds. A customer could decide to fix an old wheel instead of buying a new one. A child could turn back and apologize. A tired person could sit on a bench without feeling late.\n\nOne spring morning, a visiting clockmaker climbed the tower and corrected the mechanism. By noon, the hands matched every watch in town. The mayor praised the work. The baker offered the clockmaker a warm loaf. Mara looked up from a stubborn chain and said nothing.\n\nFor several days, people walked a little faster. They checked their pockets more often. They arrived on time and found themselves waiting for one another.\n\nThen, on a windy afternoon, the clock stopped altogether. A loose feather had somehow lodged inside its works. The clockmaker was already gone, and no one else volunteered to climb the tower.\n\nThe town adapted. People met when the shadows reached the fountain, or when the baker opened his door. Mara hung a sign outside her shop: TAKE YOUR TIME.\n\nYears later, someone repaired the clock. It ran perfectly, but by then the sign had become the town\u2019s favorite way of telling time.",
